From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 10:08:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: keep the IP length of a Wi-Fi local-in frame

The Wi-Fi local fastpath wrote the length of the received buffer into
the IP header of each frame that the PPE forced to the CPU. A frame
below the minimum Ethernet size arrives with padding, so the padding
became TCP data. A TCP ACK without data from the peer corrupted the
stream of a bound flow.

A frame of a bound Wi-Fi flow now keeps its IP length. The ethernet
driver sets the length of an LRO aggregate before the fastpath gets
the frame, so no aggregate depends on the removed code.

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/arht_wifi_lo.c |   18 ------------------
 1 file changed, 18 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/arht_wifi_lo.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/arht_wifi_lo.c
@@ -615,24 +615,6 @@ static int wifi_lo_prepare_gro(struct sk
 
 	skb->ip_summed = CHECKSUM_UNNECESSARY;
 
-	/* LRO super-packet fixup (user note): we always come in here with
-	 * LRO enabled, skb->len > MTU, and skb->data pointing at the IP
-	 * header. Directly restamp the length -- no ntohs/compare needed.
-	 * TCP checksum is skipped via CHECKSUM_UNNECESSARY (the driver's
-	 * LRO aggregator already validated per-segment TCP csums during
-	 * merging). */
-	if (likely(!ipv6)) {
-		struct iphdr *iph = (struct iphdr *)skb->data;
-
-		iph->tot_len = htons(skb->len);
-		iph->check   = 0;
-		iph->check   = ip_fast_csum((void *)iph, iph->ihl);
-	} else {
-		struct ipv6hdr *ip6h = (struct ipv6hdr *)skb->data;
-
-		ip6h->payload_len = htons(skb->len - sizeof(*ip6h));
-	}
-
 	return 1;
 
 free:
